Warehouse components — sources, E-T-L, storage, marts, and B-I.

A data warehouse is an end-to-end pipeline, and data flows through six kinds of component from left to right.

It starts with data sources — where the raw data comes from: operational databases, flat files and A-P-Is, and SaaS apps like C-R-M and E-R-P. Each has its own schema and quirks.

Next is E-T-L, the movement layer: extract from the sources, transform the data — cleaning it, removing duplicates, and conforming it to common definitions — and load it into the warehouse. A staging area holds the raw extracts before they're transformed. E-L-T just swaps the order, loading first and transforming later, and we'll come back to that.

Then storage — the warehouse database itself: the curated, integrated tables, facts and dimensions, tuned for analytical reads.

Wrapping all of it is metadata — data about the data: table and column definitions, lineage that tells you where each value came from, and the business glossary. It's the catalog that makes the warehouse understandable and governable.

From storage we carve data marts — subject-specific subsets serving one department, like sales or finance — which we'll cover next.

And on top sit the access and B-I tools: dashboards and reports, ad-hoc and O-L-A-P analysis, and data-science and machine-learning workloads. That's the consumption layer, where curated data becomes decisions.
